 ## Overview
-Dynamic and Spacetime Modeling extends static 3D Gaussian Splatting to handle time-varying scenes, enabling real-time novel view synthesis for dynamic content . It solves the problem of temporal coherence and motion representation by introducing time-dependent attributes (position, rotation, opacity) or canonical-space deformation fields, distinguishing itself from static methods that assume scene rigidity .
+Dynamic and Spacetime Modeling extends static 3D Gaussian Splatting to handle temporal variations, enabling real-time novel view synthesis for dynamic scenes . It solves the problem of modeling emerging, vanishing, and deforming content by integrating time-dependent attributes (motion, opacity, rotation) into the Gaussian representation . It is distinguished from static methods by its explicit handling of time as a dimension, using either learned deformation fields or parametric polynomial trajectories to model geometry changes .
 
 ## Shared principles
-All methods in this family share the core 3D Gaussian Splatting rendering pipeline: differentiable rasterization of 3D Gaussians projected to 2D via affine approximation [16260-deformable-3d-gaussians.md, 16313-spacetime-gaussian-feature-splatting.md]. They optimize Gaussian attributes (position, rotation, scale, opacity, features) using gradient descent (typically Adam) to minimize rendering loss (L1 + D-SSIM) against ground-truth images [16260-deformable-3d-gaussians.md, 16313-spacetime-gaussian-feature-splatting.md]. Adaptive density control (densification, pruning, splitting) is applied during training to manage Gaussian count and detail [16260-deformable-3d-gaussians.md, 16313-spacetime-gaussian-feature-splatting.md]. Initialization typically relies on Structure-from-Motion (SfM) sparse point clouds [16260-deformable-3d-gaussians.md, 16313-spacetime-gaussian-feature-splatting.md].
+All methods in this family share the core 3D Gaussian Splatting rendering pipeline: differentiable rasterization of 3D Gaussians defined by position, covariance, opacity, and color features . The covariance matrix $\Sigma$ is typically constructed from a rotation quaternion $\mathbf{r}$ and scaling vector $\mathbf{s}$ as $\Sigma = \mathbf{R}\mathbf{S}\mathbf{S}^T\mathbf{R}^T$ . Rendering uses alpha blending: $C(\mathbf{p}) = \sum_{i \in \mathcal{N}} T_i \alpha_i c_i$, where $T_i = \prod_{j=1}^{i-1} (1 - \alpha_j)$ .
+
+Key shared mechanisms include:
+1.  **Time Input:** All methods accept a time parameter $t$ to query the scene state .
+2.  **Optimization:** Training uses the Adam optimizer and minimizes a combination of L1 loss and D-SSIM loss between rendered and ground-truth images .
+3.  **Density Control:** Methods employ adaptive density control (densification, splitting, pruning) based on positional gradients and opacity thresholds to maintain representation quality .
+4.  **Initialization:** Gaussians are typically initialized from Structure-from-Motion (SfM) sparse point clouds .
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [SpacetimeGaussians Training Pipeline](15898-spacetimegaussians-training-pipeline.md)
-Explicitly models time-dependent Gaussians with motion parameters ($\omega$) and applies a structured densification/pruning schedule with motion regularization. Chose for explicit temporal attribute optimization with specific regularization terms.
+A training strategy that integrates motion parameters ($\omega$) directly into Gaussian attributes. It distinguishes itself by using a structured schedule to freeze motion parameters after initial training and applying specific regularization terms for motion gradients. Choose this for explicit motion modeling with controlled freezing.
 
 ### [Deformable 3D Gaussians](16260-deformable-3d-gaussians.md)
-Uses a canonical space with a learned deformation network (MLP) to map static Gaussians to dynamic states. Chose for monocular dynamic scene reconstruction with decoupled radiance and deformation fields.
+A framework that decouples the radiance field from a learned deformation field. It models time-varying geometry by learning offsets $(\delta\mathbf{x}, \delta\mathbf{r}, \delta\mathbf{s})$ from a canonical space using an MLP. It distinguishes itself by using a deformation network and Annealing Smooth Training (AST) to mitigate temporal jitter. Choose this for high-fidelity reconstruction of complex deformations.
 
 ### [Spacetime Gaussian Feature Splatting](16313-spacetime-gaussian-feature-splatting.md)
-Models 4D spacetime with polynomial motion/rotation and neural features, supporting emerging/vanishing content. Chose for high-fidelity dynamic scenes with explicit temporal opacity and feature splatting.
+A representation that models emerging/vanishing content using time-dependent opacity and polynomial motion/rotation. It distinguishes itself by using neural features (MLP) instead of spherical harmonics and polynomial trajectories for motion. Choose this for scenes with significant appearance changes or non-rigid motion.
 
 ## Background
-Originating from the success of 3D Gaussian Splatting [9128-3d-gaussian-splatting-implementation-details.md], dynamic variants emerged to handle video and dynamic scenes. Deformable 3D Gaussians [16260-deformable-3d-gaussians.md] and SpacetimeGaussians [15898-spacetimegaussians-training-pipeline.md] address real-time dynamic view synthesis. Spacetime Gaussian Feature Splatting [16313-spacetime-gaussian-feature-splatting.md] extends this to 4D spacetime modeling. These methods are used in dynamic scene reconstruction, novel view synthesis, and real-time rendering of moving content.
+Originating from the need to render dynamic scenes in real-time, these methods build on [3D Gaussian Splatting](9128-3d-gaussian-splatting-implementation-details.md). Deformable 3D Gaussians  addresses limitations of implicit methods (slow convergence, overfitting) by using explicit Gaussians with a deformation field. SpacetimeGaussians  and Spacetime Gaussian Feature Splatting  further explore parametric and feature-based temporal modeling.
 
